refactor(edge): dissolve ferrox-edge into the crates that use it, delete what nothing will

`ferrox-edge` was FreeToken's host-side policy meant to be INTEGRATED
into ferrox. It became a separate crate of 28384 lines, roughly half
wired. It is gone. The serving half is `ferrox-server::policy`; the MoE
expert-residency half landed in `ferrox-core` in the previous commit.

WHY MODULE-LEVEL REACHABILITY WAS THE WRONG TEST, since the measured
"external references per module" numbers that motivated this were wrong
in BOTH directions. They said `stats`, `maintenance`, `outbox`,
`footprint` and `rebuild` had zero external callers. Every one of them
is wired: the callers reach them through the crate's `pub use` facade,
so a `ferrox_edge::module::` grep never sees them. And they said
`window_pool` and `state_pool` were reachable, through `scheduler`
functions that were themselves dead.

WHAT ACTUALLY FINDS DEAD CODE is making it crate-INTERNAL and reading
`dead_code`. A published crate's `pub` items are invisible to that lint,
which is exactly how 5000+ lines sat here tested and uncalled. Moving
the modules behind `pub(crate)` turned 155 warnings on, and they are the
real inventory.

DELETED, 8098 lines, none of it reachable and none of it serving an open
roadmap item:

  cache_manager 2001 + radix::swa 970 + radix::hybrid 626
    + window_pool 397 + state_pool 807
      One closed cluster: only `cache_manager` uses the others and
      nothing uses it. It is a SECOND page allocator for a job
      `ferrox_core::cache::KvBlockPool` already does on a wired, running
      path -- `generate.rs` allocates, publishes and refcounts through
      it today. Wiring this would mean replacing a working allocator,
      not filling a gap, and it is the same "two systems that do not
      know about each other" hazard this refactor exists to remove.
  cache_report 495   a text renderer for a CacheGeometry nothing builds
  supervisor 747     the lifecycle of an engine CHILD PROCESS; ferrox
                     ships one binary and nothing implements ProcessHost
  detokenize -294    the async detokenizer worker; ferrox detokenizes
                     inline. The stop-string withhold rule and
                     floor_char_boundary, both wired, stay
  anchor -176        the recurrent-state half (PingPong, snapshot);
                     its consumers went with the cluster above
  pool -1076         the SWA/linear sizing and KV cost model, whose only
                     callers were cache_manager and state_pool

KEPT DESPITE HAVING NO CALLER, because each serves a named open item,
and each now says so at its declaration in `policy/mod.rs`:
`scheduler`'s admission and chunked prefill (`sched-time-debt`),
`radix::evict` (`wire-radix-prefix-cache`), `pool::validate_rebuild` and
`rebuild`/`maintenance`'s transitions (the /admin rebuild endpoint),
`anchor::prefill_slide` (`window-slide-during-decode`).
`grep -n "allow(dead_code)" crates/ferrox-server/src/policy/mod.rs` is
meant to read as a to-do list, not as cover.

`pool` split in two on its own seam: the byte-budget-to-slot-count
arithmetic is `ferrox_core::expert_budget`, beside the expert cache
whose slots it counts and named verbatim by `out-of-core-moe.md` as
existing and unwired. The live re-split validation stayed here.
`stats` split the same way: the two summary statistics both crates need
are `ferrox_core::summary_stats`, the serving telemetry is
`policy::serving_stats`. No module was appended to an existing large
file; every survivor is its own file under `policy/`.

TWO LIVE DEFECTS FOUND WHILE VERIFYING A DELETION, both recorded in
`docs/plans/roadmap.md` under `c3-serving-and-kv` and NOT fixed here:
`RadixCache::evict` has no caller, so nothing releases what
`generate.rs:685` retains and the page pool shrinks monotonically until
admission refuses; and `batch_scheduler` adopts from the tree but never
publishes to it, so prefix sharing under continuous batching is
adopt-only. Checked and CLEAN: the handover trap `cache_manager`
documented does not exist in `generate::publish_to_radix`, which avoids
both the leak and the read-after-free structurally.

ATTRIBUTION. FreeToken is Apache-2.0 and the notice is a licence
obligation, so `docs/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` now maps every surviving
module to its FreeToken source at its NEW path, and keeps a table of
what was ported and then deleted, with the reason. Dropping those rows
silently would have been the dishonest option.

Gated: build/test/clippy -D warnings/fmt on default, `--features metal`
and `--features cuda`, plus `--release -p ferrox-cli --features
"serve metal"` and `clippy --release`.

